Now I understand the memory model. `AllocChunk` allocates from a block pool. When `StringAppend` needs to grow, it calls `AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max)` which allocates a new chunk of size `s->max` and copies the old data. This seems fine.

But wait - there's a subtle bug. In `StringAppend`:
```c
if (s->len + 1 >= s->max)
{
    s->max *= 10;
    new_ptr = AllocChunk(s->it8, s->max);
    memcpy(new_ptr, s->begin, s->len);
    s->begin = new_ptr;
}
s->begin[s->len++] = c;
s->begin[s->len] = 0;
```

The initial `s->max = MAXSTR`. Let me check what MAXSTR is. If MAXSTR is small, then after growth, `s->max *= 10`. This seems to work. But there's a potential integer overflow in `s->max *= 10` if the string is extremely long. But that's hard to trigger.

